13
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:48,040
So you may have heard the expression about
free as in freedom not free, as in beer.

14
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:53,207
And so the word free is sort of
overloaded in the english language.

15
00:00:53,207 --> 00:00:58,458
You can be talk talking about freedom
like your abilities not being

16
00:00:58,458 --> 00:01:05,239
restricted your abilities to do things
be you're being free to do something.

17
00:01:05,239 --> 00:01:08,570
Or you can talk about
things not having any cost.

18
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:12,182
And so that's the free, as in beer where
if someone's giving away free beer,

19
00:01:12,182 --> 00:01:16,240
what they mean is they're not going to
charge you any money to get the beer.

20
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:19,236
And so what we talk about when
we're talking about licensing for

21
00:01:19,236 --> 00:01:22,131
Lennox, we're talking about
the first definition of free.

22
00:01:22,131 --> 00:01:23,420
The free as in Freedom.

23
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:27,886
So you have the freedom to obtain and you
have the freedom to modify the source code

24
00:01:27,886 --> 00:01:31,450
as long as you follow,
follow the stipulations in the license.

25
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:37,428
There are two basic categories of
licensing for open source software,

26
00:01:37,428 --> 00:01:42,430
and the first is what will refer
to as permissive licenses.

27
00:01:42,430 --> 00:01:46,023
So in other words, these are licenses
that give you the freedom to obtain and

28
00:01:46,023 --> 00:01:46,989
modify the source.

29
00:01:46,989 --> 00:01:51,817
But with relatively few limitations
that you have based on your

30
00:01:51,817 --> 00:01:54,651
modifications to that source code.

31
00:01:55,740 --> 00:02:00,930
Two examples of permissive licenses
that you'll see on open source software.

32
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:05,720
RBSD, which stands for Berkeley Software
Distribution and MITT, which stands for

33
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:08,161
massachusetts Institute of Technology.

34
00:02:08,161 --> 00:02:12,359
So these are original software licenses
that came about from each one of these

35
00:02:12,359 --> 00:02:17,640
academic institutions during the early
days of open source software development.

36
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:22,999
And the basic premise of permissive
licenses is that you can use the code,

37
00:02:22,999 --> 00:02:24,823
you can modify the code.

38
00:02:24,823 --> 00:02:28,465
And you don't necessarily need
to share those modifications or

39
00:02:28,465 --> 00:02:32,243
the source code that you incorporate
them into with your customers

40
00:02:32,243 --> 00:02:35,290
if you're developing
an embedding mimics device.

41
00:02:35,290 --> 00:02:39,318
So this is important in some scenarios
because if you're working for

42
00:02:39,318 --> 00:02:44,187
a company and they're developing their
own proprietary intellectual property.

43
00:02:44,187 --> 00:02:49,126
You wouldn't want to necessarily make
restrict the company in the way that would

44
00:02:49,126 --> 00:02:54,062
make them need to share all the details of
their intellectual property as a part of

45
00:02:54,062 --> 00:02:55,461
using some software.

46
00:02:57,740 --> 00:03:00,994
So on the other side of
permissive licensing is what's

47
00:03:00,994 --> 00:03:03,040
called copy left licensing.

48
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:05,540
And it's kind of a play
on the word copyright.

49
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:09,544
Where what it's saying is that
if you incorporate the license,

50
00:03:09,544 --> 00:03:13,412
this license material into your
work with some limitations.

51
00:03:13,412 --> 00:03:17,596
You typically need to share the content of
the overall system that you're building is

52
00:03:17,596 --> 00:03:18,361
based on that.

53
00:03:19,940 --> 00:03:24,850
So two examples are the main example
of the copy left license that's used

54
00:03:24,850 --> 00:03:28,170
with Lennox is the GPL or
the new public license.

55
00:03:28,170 --> 00:03:29,641
So what does this mean?

56
00:03:29,641 --> 00:03:34,301
It means that if you use the GPL and
you use GPL licensed software,

57
00:03:34,301 --> 00:03:37,120
your customers that buy your product.

58
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:41,693
And by virtue of the open nature
of the Internet, essentially,

59
00:03:41,693 --> 00:03:46,840
you need to plan on, everyone would
get access to the source code.

60
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:50,640
If the source code that you developed
is what's considered a derivative work.

61
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:55,461
So the definition of derivative work
involves maybe more legal terms,

62
00:03:55,461 --> 00:03:59,450
then we're going to come and
be able to cover in this course.

63
00:03:59,450 --> 00:04:03,557
But what it really means is if you're
modifying an application that's GPL

64
00:04:03,557 --> 00:04:04,217
licensed.

65
00:04:04,217 --> 00:04:08,750
You're going to have to share the content
of those modifications in order to comply

66
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:09,961
with license terms.

67
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:16,339
So, here's an example of a permissive
license in this case, an MIT license.

68
00:04:16,339 --> 00:04:22,040
So this shows some of the typical things
that you'll see in a software license.

69
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:25,711
So first it goes over your rights
as a user of the software.

70
00:04:25,711 --> 00:04:29,164
So, some of the things to look for
here, I can modify the software,

71
00:04:29,164 --> 00:04:32,090
I can distribute the software,
I can sell the software.

72
00:04:32,090 --> 00:04:36,880
So this is telling me all the things
that I can do as a user of the software.

73
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:41,171
And then this tells me what you know,
if I'm going to use the software for

74
00:04:41,171 --> 00:04:44,240
the terms of the license,
what do I have to do?

75
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:47,041
And in this case, what it says
is the above copyright notice.

76
00:04:47,041 --> 00:04:50,068
And this permission notice shall be
included in all copies are substantial

77
00:04:50,068 --> 00:04:51,190
portions of the software.

78
00:04:51,190 --> 00:04:55,320
So I need to make sure that I include
the copyright information that's

79
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:59,320
associated with the license, say,
who developed this software.

80
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:04,127
And I need to pass on
the permission notice that

81
00:05:04,127 --> 00:05:07,461
is given here in this document.

82
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:13,100
And then it also covers the limitation as
a user that you need to pass on to your

83
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:15,381
users as well for the use of the software.

84
00:05:15,381 --> 00:05:18,129
And, and the basic, you know,

85
00:05:18,129 --> 00:05:24,100
nonlegal layman's description of this
is if it doesn't work, don't sue me.

86
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:24,651
Right?
So

87
00:05:24,651 --> 00:05:29,492
what this is saying is that the copyright
holder is letting you use it for

88
00:05:29,492 --> 00:05:31,450
whatever purpose you want.

89
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:36,337
As long as you give them attribution in
the form of notifying that you're using

90
00:05:36,337 --> 00:05:40,129
their copyright and
as long as you free them from liability.

91
00:05:40,129 --> 00:05:44,476
And you pass that along to your customers
to let them know that the original soft.

92
00:05:44,476 --> 00:05:47,916
They can't go back and sue the original
copyright holder if there's something

93
00:05:47,916 --> 00:05:49,261
doesn't work in this office.

94
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:55,451
Two examples of licensing that
are used in Linux software development

95
00:05:55,451 --> 00:05:58,730
that are the copy left variety
are L G P L and G P L.

96
00:05:58,730 --> 00:06:02,561
So L GPL is typically used for
software libraries.

97
00:06:02,561 --> 00:06:07,742
So, for instance, GCC libraries and
these allow you to link to a proprietary

98
00:06:07,742 --> 00:06:12,941
application without needing to share
the source code of your applications.

99
00:06:12,941 --> 00:06:15,861
So, you see this used for
things like gcc libraries.

100
00:06:15,861 --> 00:06:20,507
This allows you to build your software
with GCC calls something like Prune F.

101
00:06:20,507 --> 00:06:25,381
And not have to worry about having
licensing restrictions apply to

102
00:06:25,381 --> 00:06:29,907
your application that you built
based on that call to a library

103
00:06:29,907 --> 00:06:33,040
that's included as part of GCC.

104
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:37,377
But what it does say is that if you needed
to make some change to a print F function

105
00:06:37,377 --> 00:06:40,158
or to something that's
a part of the GCC library.

106
00:06:40,158 --> 00:06:44,097
You would be legally obligated
to share that change

107
00:06:44,097 --> 00:06:47,861
when you distribute
the ultimate application.

108
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:54,747
So for GPL based software,
like what is used for the links,

109
00:06:54,747 --> 00:07:00,031
Colonel GPS I mentioned before is
considered a copy left license.

110
00:07:00,031 --> 00:07:04,343
In other words, if you incorporate
in any way the source code of that

111
00:07:04,343 --> 00:07:07,153
GPL licensed software into your software.

112
00:07:07,153 --> 00:07:09,503
Your software is also bound
by the terms of the GPL,

113
00:07:09,503 --> 00:07:13,540
meaning that you would need to
distribute its source code to customers.

114
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:15,171
There are different versions of the GPL.

115
00:07:15,171 --> 00:07:20,280
However, you'll see that reference in GPL,
version two versus version three.

116
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:24,981
The Tebow that we mentioned earlier as
being one of the first Lennox hardware

117
00:07:24,981 --> 00:07:26,607
devices did some change.

118
00:07:26,607 --> 00:07:31,510
Which meant that they could,
meet the spirit of the GPL or

119
00:07:31,510 --> 00:07:34,481
meet the requirements of the GPL.

120
00:07:34,481 --> 00:07:39,162
Version two by publishing all
their source code, but not allow

121
00:07:39,162 --> 00:07:44,570
the customer to modify the source code and
then run it on their hardware.

122
00:07:44,570 --> 00:07:49,446
So they put some limited restrictions and
hardware, which meant that their

123
00:07:49,446 --> 00:07:54,181
hardware could only run the specific
Lennox in binary that they created.

124
00:07:54,181 --> 00:07:58,391
And not something else that would be
created by someone else using their source

125
00:07:58,391 --> 00:08:00,240
code as a reference.

126
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:04,191
And there was some consternation in
the community about whether that was

127
00:08:04,191 --> 00:08:07,040
an appropriate and
valid use of the license.

128
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:10,171
And so
part of the newest revision of GPL or

129
00:08:10,171 --> 00:08:14,291
GPL V three specifically
prevents that that use case.

130
00:08:14,291 --> 00:08:18,788
In other words, it says that not only
must you share all your source, but

131
00:08:18,788 --> 00:08:23,287
you also must not restrict the ability
of the user to change the source and

132
00:08:23,287 --> 00:08:24,861
run it on your hardware.

133
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:30,912
However, importantly, the Lennox Colonel
has not incorporated GPL version three.

134
00:08:30,912 --> 00:08:34,337
So the Colonel still uses
the version two of the license that

135
00:08:34,337 --> 00:08:38,877
