Tebow did that shares the source code.

136
00:08:38,877 --> 00:08:42,309
But does not necessarily
make it possible for

137
00:08:42,309 --> 00:08:47,106
customers to update with their
own source code on your device.

138
00:08:47,106 --> 00:08:51,843
So what do we mean when we talk about
contributing to open source community?

139
00:08:51,843 --> 00:08:55,001
And what are some of the things that go
into contributing, open source software?

140
00:08:55,001 --> 00:08:59,091
I mentioned before that there are groups
that are associated with each software

141
00:08:59,091 --> 00:08:59,900
application.

142
00:08:59,900 --> 00:09:03,481
In terms of Lennox application
development, there's groups that

143
00:09:03,481 --> 00:09:07,205
are associated with each specific
sub component of the Linux kernel.

144
00:09:07,205 --> 00:09:08,930
And contributing to open source for

145
00:09:08,930 --> 00:09:12,844
those groups typically means that you're
going to be communicating with others,

146
00:09:12,844 --> 00:09:15,920
probably in written fashion and
most likely over open forums.

147
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:19,701
So you're going to be using things like
mailing lists to interact with a bunch of

148
00:09:19,701 --> 00:09:23,425
people that have some stake in the
development of whatever software you're

149
00:09:23,425 --> 00:09:24,640
talking about.

150
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:27,275
So this means that you're going to
need to be responding to questions

151
00:09:27,275 --> 00:09:28,000
about your code.

152
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,610
You're going to need to ask good
questions when you need help from others.

153
00:09:31,610 --> 00:09:33,756
And you're going to have
to follow detailed and

154
00:09:33,756 --> 00:09:36,523
specific submission rules about
how to submit changes for

155
00:09:36,523 --> 00:09:40,040
whatever project you happen to
be trying to contribute to.

156
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:44,845
And I want you to think about making open
source contributions as especially as

157
00:09:44,845 --> 00:09:46,624
a part of your final project.

158
00:09:46,624 --> 00:09:51,471
And how you could maybe leverage
that in terms of advertising your,

159
00:09:51,471 --> 00:09:52,711
your own skills.

160
00:09:52,711 --> 00:09:55,722
So this is the kind of thing that you
should be able to ultimately put on your

161
00:09:55,722 --> 00:09:58,551
resume, the fact that you contributed
to some open source project.

162
00:10:00,340 --> 00:10:02,099
And each, as I mentioned before,

163
00:10:02,099 --> 00:10:05,090
each project has their own
rules about how to contribute.

